### The Foundation of Every JS Program

Before you can write any JavaScript, you need to understand how variables work — not just the syntax, but what's actually happening under the hood. JavaScript has three ways to declare variables, and the differences between them affect scoping, hoisting, and safety.

#### `var`, `let`, and `const`

```javascript
// var: function-scoped, hoisted, can be re-declared (avoid in modern code)
var score = 10;
var score = 20;     // No error — silently re-declares. This is a bug magnet.

// let: block-scoped, cannot be re-declared in the same scope
let count = 0;
count = 1;           // OK: reassignment is fine
// let count = 2;    // SyntaxError: already declared

// const: block-scoped, cannot be reassigned (but objects/arrays can be mutated)
const PI = 3.14159;
// PI = 3;           // TypeError: assignment to constant variable

const user = { name: 'Alice' };
user.name = 'Bob';   // OK — mutating the object, not reassigning the binding
// user = {};        // TypeError: can't reassign

const nums = [1, 2, 3];
nums.push(4);        // OK — mutating the array is fine
```

**The rule**: Default to `const`. Only use `let` when you know you'll reassign. Never use `var` — it was designed before block scoping existed and its behavior is confusing.

#### Block Scope vs Function Scope

Scope is the region of code where a variable is accessible.

```javascript
function example() {
  var functionScoped = 'I\'m available anywhere in this function';

  if (true) {
    var alsoFunctionScoped = 'var ignores blocks!';  // leaks out
    let blockScoped = 'only inside this if block';
    const alsoBlock = 'same';
  }

  console.log(functionScoped);       // ✓ 'I\'m available...'
  console.log(alsoFunctionScoped);   // ✓ var leaked out of the if block
  // console.log(blockScoped);       // ✗ ReferenceError
}

// Classic var bug in loops
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}
// Prints: 3, 3, 3 — all closures share the same var i

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}
// Prints: 0, 1, 2 — each iteration gets its own let binding
```

#### Hoisting

JavaScript processes variable and function declarations before executing code. This is called **hoisting**.

```javascript
// var declarations are hoisted (but not their values)
console.log(x);  // undefined — not a ReferenceError!
var x = 5;

// What actually happens behind the scenes:
var x;           // declaration moved to top of function
console.log(x);  // undefined
x = 5;           // assignment stays in place

// let and const are hoisted, but live in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)
// Accessing them before the declaration line throws an error
console.log(y);  // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'y' before initialization
let y = 10;

// Function DECLARATIONS are fully hoisted — can be called before they appear
greet('Alice');  // Works!
function greet(name) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}

// Function EXPRESSIONS are NOT fully hoisted
hello();         // TypeError: hello is not a function
var hello = function() { console.log('Hello!'); };
// var hello is hoisted as undefined, so hello() is undefined()
```

#### The Scope Chain

When you access a variable, JavaScript searches from the current scope outward until it finds it or hits global scope:

```javascript
const globalVar = 'global';

function outer() {
  const outerVar = 'outer';

  function inner() {
    const innerVar = 'inner';

    // JavaScript searches: inner → outer → global
    console.log(innerVar);  // found in inner
    console.log(outerVar);  // found in outer
    console.log(globalVar); // found in global
  }

  // console.log(innerVar); // ReferenceError — inner not accessible here
  inner();
}

outer();
```

